    "summary": "Horn‑driven Weather Report Suite and fiery Sugar Magnolia",
    "blurb": "The second set bursts with horn‑charged jams, highlighted by an expansive Weather Report Suite and a Theremin‑tinged Looks Like Rain. Jerry delivers powerful vocals on Sugar Magnolia while Keith’s keyboard work shines throughout. Overall the show offers strong performances despite a few rough spots in the first set.",
    "review": "On September 17 1973 at the Onondaga County War Memorial, the Dead unleashed one of their quintessential early‑'70s funk‑jazz outings. The first set rolls through familiar material—Tennessee Jed, Sugaree, and a loopy Looks Like Rain featuring Keith’s surprising Theremin solo—but suffers from a few level hiccups (notably on Loser) and a couple of lyrical slips. The real magic arrives in the second set: a horn‑augmented Weather Report Suite that swells through its Prelude, Part 1, and Let It Grow before spilling into a soulful Stella Blue and an exuberant Sugar Magnolia. Eyes Of The World rides the groove with tight interplay, and Loose Lucy provides a loose‑feeling encore of pure jam energy. Jerry’s vocal presence is “evil carnival” fierce on Sugar Magnolia, while Bob’s rhythm stays solid even when his words wobble. Keith shines both in the Theremin moment and his jam on Truckin’, weaving seamlessly with the brass. The soundboard capture (gd1973-09-17.sbd.sirmick.107542.flac16) preserves the crisp 1973 mix, delivering the horn texture and band dynamics with clarity despite minor tape quirks.",
